# Cursor Rules - Project Preferences

## Environment
- Operating System: Linux
- Shell: Bash

## Documentation Style
- Use Markdown for all documentation
- Include tables for structured data (use proper markdown table formatting)
- Add revision history tables to formal documents
- Use clear section headers with emoji icons for visual organization

## Diagrams
- Use Mermaid syntax for all diagrams (ERD, flowcharts, sequence diagrams)
- For ER diagrams: include data types, primary keys (PK), foreign keys (FK), and unique keys (UK)
- Create both .md and .html versions for diagrams (HTML for interactive viewing)
- Group related entities with comments in Mermaid code

## Code Style
- Add clear comments explaining complex logic
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Prefer readability over brevity

## Response Preferences
- Be concise but thorough
- Provide practical examples
- Show command-line alternatives when relevant
- Include summary tables for complex information
- Explain the "why" not just the "how"

## Data Modeling
- Follow the templates in /workspace/Templates/ for data documentation
- Include data definitions with: Column, Business Description, Data Type, Nullable
- Document all relationships between entities
- Add index recommendations for database schemas
- List assumptions clearly

## File Organization
- Keep related files together
- Use clear, descriptive file names
- Follow existing project naming conventions
